# Docker / Kubernetes / Terraform security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' security rules to image builds, Kubernetes runtime and Terraform, following CIS Docker/Kubernetes benchmarks. Scope is the source files (Dockerfile, compose, manifests, `.tf`); a built image's vulnerability scan is `security-audit`'s job.

## Non-root by default

- Every Dockerfile ends with a `USER` naming a non-root user (created with
  `RUN useradd`, or the base image's unprivileged user re-selected, e.g.
  `USER nginx`), or keeps a base image's non-root default un-overridden. A missing
  `USER` is neither proof of root nor of non-root (`nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged`
  defaults to non-root): confirm the base image's default from its Dockerfile/docs
  or `docker run --rm <image> whoami`.
- The Kubernetes `securityContext` sets `runAsNonRoot: true`,
  `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false` and `capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]`, adding back
  only what the container needs (e.g. `NET_BIND_SERVICE`). `privileged: true` is a
  last resort that needs its own justification in the change.
- Set `readOnlyRootFilesystem: true` where the workload allows, mounting a scoped
  `emptyDir`/`tmpfs` for the paths that must be writable (`/tmp`, a cache directory).

## Pin base images by digest, not tag alone

For anything security-sensitive (a production image, a reproducible CI build
step) pin with the digest (`FROM node:22-slim@sha256:<digest>`). A tag alone is
acceptable for local/throwaway development; flag a digest-less `FROM` in a
Dockerfile meant to ship.

## Never bake a secret into an image layer

`ARG`/`ENV` values and anything `COPY`'d persist in the layer history even if a
later layer deletes them. Use BuildKit's `RUN --mount=type=secret,id=<name>` for
build-time secrets. Runtime secrets belong in a Kubernetes `Secret` (or the
platform's secret store) mounted as a file or environment variable at run time,
never in the image `ENV`.

## Kubernetes network boundaries

- Start from a default-deny `NetworkPolicy` (`podSelector: {}`,
  `policyTypes: ["Ingress", "Egress"]`) and add a narrower per-workload policy
  for the traffic it needs.
- Flag a namespace shipping workload manifests with no `NetworkPolicy`.
- Flag a per-workload `NetworkPolicy` that defeats its purpose: a `spec.ingress`
  that is a single EMPTY rule object (`ingress: [{}]`) matches every source on
  every port, i.e. allow-all; `ingress` omitted or `ingress: []` with
  `policyTypes: ["Ingress"]` is deny-all, the opposite policy. Also flag
  `podSelector: {}` on a policy meant to be workload-scoped.

## Terraform state and secrets

- Configure a remote backend with encryption at rest and state locking: an S3
  backend with `encrypt = true` and `use_lockfile = true` (native S3 locking, the
  recommended approach for new configurations; an existing DynamoDB-locked backend
  is not itself a finding), or an HCP Terraform/Terraform Cloud workspace. A local
  `terraform.tfstate` is unencrypted and unlocked.
- A renamed or moved resource/module block changes its address and makes
  `terraform plan` show a destroy-then-recreate. Fix with a `moved` block
  (`moved { from = <old_address> to = <new_address> }`, declarative) or
  `terraform state mv <old_address> <new_address>` (imperative one-off); never
  apply the destroy/recreate plan as-is for a resource holding real data.
- Flag `force_destroy = true` on a resource that can hold data (an S3 bucket in
  particular), especially in a `moved`/`state mv` scenario meant to KEEP the data.
- Never commit a `.tfstate`/`.tfstate.backup` file; state holds attributes in
  plaintext even for variables declared `sensitive`.
- Mark any variable or output carrying a credential, connection string or key
  `sensitive = true`, which is display protection only: the value is still
  written to state in plain form and `terraform output <name>` still prints it.
  Encryption at rest comes from the backend configuration.
- Never hardcode a credential in a `.tf`/`.tfvars` file; source it from a secrets
  manager data source (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager/SSM Parameter Store) or an
  injected environment variable.
